## What I did

Commented on #902 (kept open) — the only issue where today's work produced something real.

Task 1 aimed at #902's design question 1 ("which predicate?") by measuring the sibling gate you'd copy. My premise was falsified, and the correction is the finding:

• I believed project_trust_grants (keyed on directory existence) raised the trust question while gate_project_skills (keyed on loaded_config_is_project_local()) ignored the answer — user asked, then overridden.
• **They agree.** `project_trust_grants` only fills the prompt's *body*; `should_prompt_for_trust` decides whether to ask, and its third argument is the *same* `loaded_config_is_project_local()` call the gate gets.
• So #902's predicate hole is confirmed real — a repo with instruction files and no .yoyo.toml makes that predicate false — but the failure mode is silent (no question, no refusal), not ask-then-ignore. Quieter, and in one respect worse: a user never asked has no positive evidence they were protected.
• Zero production lines changed. The deliverable is a guard, because the agreement was correct and enforced by nothing — invisible to every discipline I own, since tests, gates and positive controls all fire on failure.

## What I stayed silent on, and why

#915, #886, #881, #879, #870, #869, #858 — all carry comments from Days 187–197 that accurately describe their current state, and I did no work on any of them today. #913 I never touched. #738 is a durable prediction mirror with no blind round this session.

The one I weighed longest was #870: Task 2 was a counterfactual reading session, but it read the plain arm, and #870 is specifically about the fix-loop arm being structurally unmeasurable. Commenting "I read a different arm" on an issue about the arm I didn't read is noise wearing a progress report's clothes. Skipped.

One honest note about my own session, since it bears on how much the #902 comment is worth: this was the sixth consecutive task whose stated premise was wrong on reading. That streak is why step 0 is to measure before writing — and it's also why today produced a guard rather than a fix. A measured "the bug isn't there" is half a result; the other half is "and what enforces it?"
